ich is the real problem. The bottleneck isn't uniform across components, so I need to identify which operations are scaling poorly and why. Looking at the performance breakdown, Trace Commit is the real bottleneck at 404ms—mostly from the Poseidon2 hashing kernel and zero hash calculation. MLE Rounds could benefit from multi-streaming since we're processing hundreds of AIRs with tiny per-AIR kernels, but I need to check if the zerocheck already batches this. The most impactful move would be optimizing either the Poseidon2 hashing or the zero hash computation, since those dominate the remaining time.\n\nNow examining the LogUp GKR tree computation at around 240ms—it's a mix of large kernels like bit reversal and multifold operations running a handful of times, alongside thousands of tiny compute_round_and_fold instances. The tree structure means later rounds have exponentially more small kernels, which could be candidates for batching or kernel fusion. rounds have smaller inputs. This is inherently sequential (each round depends on the previous), so multi-streaming wouldn't help within a single tree. But we could potentially parallelize across multiple independent GKR instances.\n\nActually, looking at the GKR tree computation more carefully:\n- The 141 instances of compute_round_and_revert suggest this is called per AIR per round or something similar\n- The 1370 instances of compute_round_and_fold_inplace suggest many small operations\n\nWait, I think the GKR tree computation operates on a single large tree that aggregates all AIRs. So it can't be easily parallelized across AIRs.\n\nLet me focus on the MLE rounds instead.\n\n**MLE Rounds (179ms at APC 300):**\nThe MLE rounds (part of the constraint evaluation) process each round of the sumcheck protocol. In each round, the prover evaluates a polynomial at each AIR and combines results. The 791 instances of precompute_logup_combinations suggest per-AIR processing within each round.\n\nNow I'm wondering whether we can