{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015ooFuXo7vznHmWRj2aF1CR","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information I need. Let me create a comprehensive report.\n\n## Summary: GKR Tree Computation Code Architecture\n\nBased on my thorough analysis of the codebase, here's the complete understanding of the GKR tree computation:\n\n### 1. **Entry Point for GKR Tree Computation**\n\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs` (lines 491-1089)\n\n**Function:** `pub fn fractional_sumcheck_gpu<SC, TS>(...)`\n\nThis is the main entry point that orchestrates the entire GKR tree computation after input evaluation. It:\n- Takes device buffer of leaf fractions (`Frac<EF>` = `{p: EF, q: EF}`)\n- Returns `FracSumcheckProof` containing fractional sum, per-layer claims, and sumcheck polynomials\n\n### 2. **Main Loop Structure: Two Phases**\n\n#### **Phase 1: Segment Tree Build** (Lines 517-577)\nBefore any sumcheck processing, the code builds the complete segment tree in bit-reversal order:\n\n1. **Bit-reversal + Fused layers 0+1** (Lines 525-557):\n   - For N > 1024: Call `bit_rev_frac_ext_build_k2` (kernel: `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel`)\n     - Performs bit-reversal permutation AND builds tree layers 0 & 1 in a single kernel pass\n     - Uses Z-tile shmem strategy with 256 threads, 64KB shared memory\n     - Applies alpha to denominators during layer 0\n     - Returns `start_layer_i = 2`\n   - For N ≤ 1024: Fallback to separate `bit_rev_frac_ext`, then `frac_build_tree_layer`\n\n2. **Consecutive pair fusion** (Lines 560-568):\n   - Loop: `while i + 1 < total_rounds:`\n   - Call `frac_build_tree_two_layers` (kernel: `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel`)\n     - Fuses two consecutive tree layers (i and i+1) in one pass\n     - Saves ~33% memory traffic by avoiding intermediate layer writes\n     - Preserves right-half nodes needed for revert operations\n     - Advances `i += 2`\n\n3. **Remaining odd layer** (Lines 570-575):\n   - If `i < total_rounds`: Call `frac_build_tree_layer` (kernel: `frac_build_tree_layer_kernel<false>`)\n\n4. **Root extraction & first revert** (Lines 578-606):\n   - **D2H Copy #1** (Line 579): Copy root value from device to host\n     - `copy_from_device(&layer, 0, &mut copy_scratch)`\n     - Single `EF` pair (16 bytes for BN254)\n   - Revert root layer: `frac_build_tree_layer(..., revert=true)` on size 2\n   - Observe root in transcript\n   - **D2H Copy #2** (Lines 599-600): Copy first two children (p̂_1(0), q̂_1(0), p̂_1(1), q̂_1(1))\n   - Observe claims and sample μ_1\n\n**Kernels Used:**\n- `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel` (49ms, 6 instances)\n- `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel` (11.8ms, 66 instances)\n- `frac_build_tree_layer_kernel` (implicit in two-layer kernel)\n\n---\n\n#### **Phase 2: GKR Rounds (Sumcheck + Fold Rounds)** (Lines 655-1079)\n\nLoop: `for round in 1..total_rounds:`\n\nEach GKR round `j = round + 1` consists of:\n\n**Setup:**\n- Build `SqrtEqLayers` from `xi_prev[1..]` (CPU side, device upload happens inside kernels)\n- Sample `lambda` from transcript (Fiat-Shamir)\n- Choose strategy: `precompute_m_enabled()` and parameters\n- Initialize accumulators: `prev_s_eval` (previous round's evaluation), `eq_r_acc` (accumulated eq products)\n\n**Round 0 (First Sumcheck Round):**\n\nCall `do_sumcheck_round_and_revert` (Lines 698-711):\n```\nKernel: compute_round_and_revert_kernel (69ms, 141 instances)\n```\n- **Fused kernel** combining two operations:\n  1. Revert tree layer in-place: `layer[i] = frac_unadd(layer[i], layer[i+half])`\n  2. Compute s'_0(1) and s'_0(2) directly\n- Loads pairs from layer and second-half (preserved for revert)\n- Computes eq weights from sqrt-decomposed buffer\n- Outputs: `d_sum_evals` (2 EF values = s'(1) and s'(2))\n- Block reduction writes to `tmp_block_sums[block_idx * 2 + {0,1}]`\n\n**CPU Work (Line 331):**\n\nCall `observe_and_update` → `reconstruct_s_evals` (Lines 1126-1163):\n- **D2H Copy #3+**: Transfer `d_sum_evals` from device to host (2 × 16 bytes)\n  ```rust\n  let sp_vec = d_sum_evals.to_host()?;  // sp_evals[1:] = s'(1), s'(2)\n  ```\n- **Polynomial reconstruction (CPU):**\n  - Receive s'(1) and s'(2) from GPU\n  - Scale by `eq_r_acc` (accumulated eq products from previous rounds)\n  - Derive s'(0) using constraint: `s(0) + s(1) = s_{prev}(r_prev)`\n  - Interpolate s'(3) from three points using Lagrange\n  - Compute full s(i) = eq(ξ_j, i) × s'(i) for i ∈ {1,2,3}\n- **Transcript operations:**\n  - Observe s(1), s(2), s(3) in Fiat-Shamir transcript\n  - Sample challenge ρ_0\n- **Update accumulators:**\n  - Compute eq(ξ_j, ρ_0) and multiply into `eq_r_acc`\n  - Compute next evaluation: `prev_s_eval = eq_r_acc × interpolate_quadratic(s'_vals, ρ_0)`\n- **Return:** r_0\n\n**Rounds 1..(round-1) (Subsequent Sumcheck Rounds):**\n\nTwo strategies, selected by `choose_round_strategy`:\n\n##### **Strategy 1: Fold-Eval (Default/Fallback)**\n\nFor each remaining variable in `xi_prev[1..]`:\n\nA. **Ping-pong scheduling** (lines 725-819):\n   - `BufferScheduler` decides: LayerToWork, WorkToLayer, InPlace_Layer, or InPlace_Work\n   - Goal: preserve `layer` for next GKR round's reverts (non-last rounds)\n\nB. **Fused Kernel Launch:**\n\n   **Out-of-place variant** (`do_fused_sumcheck_round`):\n   ```\n   Kernel: compute_round_and_fold_kernel\n   ```\n   - Reads: `src_pq_buffer` (size 2×pq_size), folding info from `r_prev`\n   - Writes: `dst_pq_buffer` (size pq_size), `block_sums`\n   - Fuses two operations:\n     1. **Fold** by r_prev: each pair → `even + r_prev × (odd - even)`, halving buffer\n     2. **Compute** s'_t(1) and s'_t(2) from freshly folded values\n   - No __restrict__ needed (distinct read/write buffers)\n\n   **In-place variant** (`do_fused_sumcheck_round_inplace`):\n   ```\n   Kernel: compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel (8.8ms, 1370 instances)\n   ```\n   - Reads and writes same buffer (first half written from both halves read)\n   - Same logic as out-of-place\n\nC. **CPU Work (inside `observe_and_update`):**\n   - D2H copy of `d_sum_evals` (2 EF values)\n   - Reconstruct s(1), s(2), s(3) as in Round 0\n   - Observe in transcript, sample ρ_t, update accumulators\n\nD. **Buffer progression:**\n   - `pq_size >>= 1` for next iteration\n   - Data location tracked by `scheduler.data_in_work_buffer`\n\n**Final fold** (after last sumcheck round, lines 795-819):\n   ```\n   Kernel: fold_ef_columns_kernel (if needed)\n   ```\n   - Standalone fold on final pq_size (no fused compute)\n   - No block reduction needed\n\n---\n\n##### **Strategy 2: Precompute-M (Lines 821-1054)**\n\nFor windows of w=3 sumcheck rounds (if conditions met):\n\n**Step 1: Build M matrix** (lines 909-926):\n```\nKernel: precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<inline_fold={true,false}> (45ms, 14 instances)\n+ precompute_m_reduce_partials_kernel\n```\n- Option A (inline_fold=true, first window): Folds by r_prev while building\n- Option B (inline_fold=false, subsequent): Assumes already folded\n- Computes M_{α,β}[u,v] for α,β ∈ {0,1} and u,v ∈ {0,1}^w\n- Parallelized by tail dimension (rem_n - w variables)\n- Each block produces partial M block, then reduce to final M matrix\n\n**Step 2: Eval w rounds** (lines 928-966):\n```\nKernel: precompute_m_eval_round_kernel\n```\n- Small kernel operating on 4^w = 64 element M matrix\n- For each round t ∈ [0, w):\n  - Compute eq_r_prefix from window challenges sampled so far\n  - Compute eq_suffix from remaining xi values\n  - Extract s'_t(1) and s'_t(2) from M using Lagrange/multilinearity\n  \n**CPU Work (inside loop):**\n   - **D2H Copy #4+**: Transfer `d_sum_evals` (2 EF)\n   - Reconstruct s(1), s(2), s(3)\n   - Observe in transcript, sample r_t\n   - Update `eq_r_acc` and `prev_s_eval`\n   - Accumulate challenges: `window_rs.push(r)`\n\n**Step 3: Multifold** (lines 968-1000):\n```\nKernel: multifold_kernel<4> (47ms, 14 instances)\n```\n- Folds w (or w+1 with pending) coordinates at once\n- Computes eq_r_window = MLE table of deferred challenges\n- Reads from layer_read_ptr (or active_pq if not pending)\n- Writes to active_pq\n- Reduces pq_size by 2^w (or 2^(w+1) if pending)\n\n**Fallback to fold-eval** (lines 1002-1046):\n- Any remaining rounds (rem_n < threshold) use standard fold-eval\n\n---\n\n**End of GKR Round:**\n\nAfter all sumcheck rounds:\n- **D2H Copy #5+**: Copy final two values from active buffer (lines 1057-1060)\n  ```rust\n  pq_host[0] = copy_from_device(active, 0, ...)?;\n  pq_host[1] = copy_from_device(active, pq_size/2, ...)?;\n  ```\n- Store claims for next GKR round\n- Observe claims in transcript\n- Sample μ_j (next round's reduction factor)\n- Update `xi_prev = [μ_j, ...r_vec]`\n- Accumulate `sumcheck_polys`\n\n---\n\n### 3. **CPU Work Between Kernel Launches**\n\n**Per Kernel Launch (3 main tasks):**\n\n1. **Polynomial Reconstruction** (~1-10 µs per round)\n   - D2H copy of 2 EF values from GPU (16 bytes)\n   - Field arithmetic:\n     - Inversion: `(1 - xi_j)^{-1}`\n     - Lagrange interpolation on 3 points\n     - 2 multiplications + 1 addition for eq evaluation\n     - Quadratic interpolation for next `prev_s_eval`\n\n2. **Fiat-Shamir Transcript Operations** (~1-5 µs per round)\n   - `transcript.observe_ext(3 values)` — hash update\n   - `transcript.sample_ext()` — challenge derivation from hash state\n\n3. **Buffer Management** (~negligible)\n   - Update pointers, sizes, accumulators\n   - Scheduler decisions for ping-pong\n\n**Key Insight:** CPU work is dominated by Fiat-Shamir hashing and D2H copies. Field arithmetic on CPU is minimal.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Data Dependencies Between Rounds/Levels**\n\n**Within a GKR Round (Sumcheck):**\n- Round t depends on: challenge ρ_{t-1} sampled in round t-1\n- pq_buffer must be folded by ρ_{t-1} before computing round t\n- No backward dependencies (except revert, which happens at start)\n\n**Between GKR Rounds:**\n- Round j depends on: μ_j and ρ_vec from round j-1\n- Layer buffer must be reverted at start of round j to expose layer j's data\n- eq_buffer must be rebuilt from xi_prev[1..] (CPU side, no GPU dependency)\n\n**Kernel → Kernel Serialization:**\n- Each `observe_and_update` requires D2H copy before next kernel\n- Creates hard synchronization point (CPU stall waiting for GPU result)\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Where `compute_round_and_revert` vs `compute_round_and_fold_inplace` are Used**\n\n| Kernel | Usage | Rounds Affected |\n|--------|-------|-----------------|\n| `compute_round_and_revert` | First inner round (t=0) of GKR round, after tree revert | Every GKR round |\n| `compute_round_and_fold_inplace` | Subsequent inner rounds (t=1...) using in-place strategy | Fold-eval strategy, non-last rounds |\n| `compute_round_and_fold` (out-of-place) | Subsequent inner rounds using ping-pong strategy | Fold-eval strategy, last round |\n| `precompute_m_eval_round` | Each window eval round (t=0..w-1) | Precompute-M strategy |\n\n---\n\n### 6. **How `bit_rev_frac_build_k2` Relates to Tree Structure**\n\n- **What it does:** Fused bit-reversal + tree layers 0 & 1\n- **Input:** Leaves in natural order with alpha not yet applied\n- **Output:** First 2 tree layers (halving tree from N → N/2 → N/4 nodes) in bit-reversed layout\n- **Memory benefits:** Eliminates 1.5N reads (normal: 1 read per bitrev + 1 per layer; fused: 1 read for both)\n- **Relation to tree:** After this kernel, `layer` buffer holds:\n  - `[0..N/4)`: layer 2 nodes (parents of layer 1)\n  - `[N/4..N/2)`: layer 1 nodes (preserved for revert of layer 2)\n  - `[N/2..N)`: layer 0 (leaves with alpha, preserved for revert of layer 1)\n- **Next kernels:** Continue building from layer 2 upward with `frac_build_tree_two_layers`\n\n---\n\n### 7. **D2H Copy Locations**\n\n| Line | Location | Data | Size | Frequency |\n|------|----------|------|------|-----------|\n| 579 | Tree build | Root fraction (p, q) | 32 B | Once |\n| 599-600 | GKR round 1 init | Layer 1 children (4 EF) | 64 B | Once |\n| 1057-1060 | GKR round end | Final claims (4 EF) | 64 B | Per GKR round |\n| Inside `reconstruct_s_evals` | Per sumcheck round | s'(1), s'(2) | 32 B | **Per sumcheck round** |\n\n**Total D2H per GKR round j:** 32B (final claims) + 32B × (j-1) (sumcheck rounds) = O(j × 32B)\n\nFor a large j, this is ~512 bytes per sumcheck round, which is minimal bandwidth but creates synchronization serialization.\n\n---\n\n### Files Summary\n\n**Rust Orchestration:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs` (1185 lines)\n- `fractional_sumcheck_gpu`: Main entry point\n- Helper functions: `observe_and_update`, `reconstruct_s_evals`, `copy_from_device`, `reduce_to_single_evaluation`\n- Buffer scheduling: `BufferScheduler`, strategy selection\n\n**CUDA Kernels:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr.cu` (1452 lines)\n- `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel`: Bitrev + tree layers 0-1\n- `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel`: Fused pair of tree layers\n- `compute_round_and_revert_kernel`: Revert + first sumcheck round\n- `compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel`: Fused fold + compute (in-place)\n- `compute_round_and_fold_kernel`: Fused fold + compute (out-of-place)\n- `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<bool>`: Build M matrix\n- `precompute_m_eval_round_kernel`: Evaluate M matrix for one round\n- `multifold_kernel<W>`: Multi-coordinate fold\n\n**Wrapper Layer:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` (lines 101-850)\n- C++ extern declarations and safe Rust wrappers\n\n**Documentation:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/docs/cuda-backend/gkr-prover.md` (347 lines)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b26f810-a2da-49ad-94ae-b745d8ecf3f5","uuid":"c90515a2-1164-4eae-8f30-ba62afd8c68c","timestamp":"2026-04-12T23:45:51.194Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand the GKR tree computation code in the CUDA backend of /home/georg/stark-backend.\n\nThe LogUp GKR phase has two parts:\n1. Input evaluation (per-AIR, already multi-streamed across 4 threads)\n2. Tree computation (sequential, runs after input eval)\n\nI need to understand part 2 - the tree computation. The relevant kernels from nsight profiling are:\n- fractional_sumcheck_gkr::compute_round_and_revert_kernel (69ms, 141 instances)\n- fractional_sumcheck_gkr::bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel (49ms, 6 instances)\n- fractional_sumcheck_gkr::multifold_kernel<4> (47ms, 14 instances)\n- fractional_sumcheck_gkr::precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<true,3> (45ms, 14 instances)\n- fractional_sumcheck_gkr::compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel (8.8ms, 1370 instances)\n- fractional_sumcheck_gkr::frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel (11.8ms, 66 instances)\n\nI need to find:\n1. The entry point for the GKR tree computation (after input eval completes)\n2. The main loop structure: how does it iterate through tree levels and sumcheck rounds?\n3. What CPU work happens between kernel launches? (transcript hashing, polynomial evaluation, etc.)\n4. Are there D2H copies between kernel launches?\n5. What data dependencies exist between rounds/levels?\n6. Where is `compute_round_and_revert` called vs `compute_round_and_fold_inplace`?\n7. How does `bit_rev_frac_build_k2` relate to the tree structure?\n\nFocus on files in crates/cuda-backend/src/ related to \"gkr\", \"fractional_sumcheck\", \"frac\". Also check crates/stark-backend/src/ for the GKR protocol orchestration.\n\nBe very thorough - I need to understand every step between kernel launches to identify where CPU overhead comes from.","agentId":"a31bba270dfbceeb4","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have all the information I need. Let me create a comprehensive report.\n\n## Summary: GKR Tree Computation Code Architecture\n\nBased on my thorough analysis of the codebase, here's the complete understanding of the GKR tree computation:\n\n### 1. **Entry Point for GKR Tree Computation**\n\n**File:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs` (lines 491-1089)\n\n**Function:** `pub fn fractional_sumcheck_gpu<SC, TS>(...)`\n\nThis is the main entry point that orchestrates the entire GKR tree computation after input evaluation. It:\n- Takes device buffer of leaf fractions (`Frac<EF>` = `{p: EF, q: EF}`)\n- Returns `FracSumcheckProof` containing fractional sum, per-layer claims, and sumcheck polynomials\n\n### 2. **Main Loop Structure: Two Phases**\n\n#### **Phase 1: Segment Tree Build** (Lines 517-577)\nBefore any sumcheck processing, the code builds the complete segment tree in bit-reversal order:\n\n1. **Bit-reversal + Fused layers 0+1** (Lines 525-557):\n   - For N > 1024: Call `bit_rev_frac_ext_build_k2` (kernel: `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel`)\n     - Performs bit-reversal permutation AND builds tree layers 0 & 1 in a single kernel pass\n     - Uses Z-tile shmem strategy with 256 threads, 64KB shared memory\n     - Applies alpha to denominators during layer 0\n     - Returns `start_layer_i = 2`\n   - For N ≤ 1024: Fallback to separate `bit_rev_frac_ext`, then `frac_build_tree_layer`\n\n2. **Consecutive pair fusion** (Lines 560-568):\n   - Loop: `while i + 1 < total_rounds:`\n   - Call `frac_build_tree_two_layers` (kernel: `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel`)\n     - Fuses two consecutive tree layers (i and i+1) in one pass\n     - Saves ~33% memory traffic by avoiding intermediate layer writes\n     - Preserves right-half nodes needed for revert operations\n     - Advances `i += 2`\n\n3. **Remaining odd layer** (Lines 570-575):\n   - If `i < total_rounds`: Call `frac_build_tree_layer` (kernel: `frac_build_tree_layer_kernel<false>`)\n\n4. **Root extraction & first revert** (Lines 578-606):\n   - **D2H Copy #1** (Line 579): Copy root value from device to host\n     - `copy_from_device(&layer, 0, &mut copy_scratch)`\n     - Single `EF` pair (16 bytes for BN254)\n   - Revert root layer: `frac_build_tree_layer(..., revert=true)` on size 2\n   - Observe root in transcript\n   - **D2H Copy #2** (Lines 599-600): Copy first two children (p̂_1(0), q̂_1(0), p̂_1(1), q̂_1(1))\n   - Observe claims and sample μ_1\n\n**Kernels Used:**\n- `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel` (49ms, 6 instances)\n- `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel` (11.8ms, 66 instances)\n- `frac_build_tree_layer_kernel` (implicit in two-layer kernel)\n\n---\n\n#### **Phase 2: GKR Rounds (Sumcheck + Fold Rounds)** (Lines 655-1079)\n\nLoop: `for round in 1..total_rounds:`\n\nEach GKR round `j = round + 1` consists of:\n\n**Setup:**\n- Build `SqrtEqLayers` from `xi_prev[1..]` (CPU side, device upload happens inside kernels)\n- Sample `lambda` from transcript (Fiat-Shamir)\n- Choose strategy: `precompute_m_enabled()` and parameters\n- Initialize accumulators: `prev_s_eval` (previous round's evaluation), `eq_r_acc` (accumulated eq products)\n\n**Round 0 (First Sumcheck Round):**\n\nCall `do_sumcheck_round_and_revert` (Lines 698-711):\n```\nKernel: compute_round_and_revert_kernel (69ms, 141 instances)\n```\n- **Fused kernel** combining two operations:\n  1. Revert tree layer in-place: `layer[i] = frac_unadd(layer[i], layer[i+half])`\n  2. Compute s'_0(1) and s'_0(2) directly\n- Loads pairs from layer and second-half (preserved for revert)\n- Computes eq weights from sqrt-decomposed buffer\n- Outputs: `d_sum_evals` (2 EF values = s'(1) and s'(2))\n- Block reduction writes to `tmp_block_sums[block_idx * 2 + {0,1}]`\n\n**CPU Work (Line 331):**\n\nCall `observe_and_update` → `reconstruct_s_evals` (Lines 1126-1163):\n- **D2H Copy #3+**: Transfer `d_sum_evals` from device to host (2 × 16 bytes)\n  ```rust\n  let sp_vec = d_sum_evals.to_host()?;  // sp_evals[1:] = s'(1), s'(2)\n  ```\n- **Polynomial reconstruction (CPU):**\n  - Receive s'(1) and s'(2) from GPU\n  - Scale by `eq_r_acc` (accumulated eq products from previous rounds)\n  - Derive s'(0) using constraint: `s(0) + s(1) = s_{prev}(r_prev)`\n  - Interpolate s'(3) from three points using Lagrange\n  - Compute full s(i) = eq(ξ_j, i) × s'(i) for i ∈ {1,2,3}\n- **Transcript operations:**\n  - Observe s(1), s(2), s(3) in Fiat-Shamir transcript\n  - Sample challenge ρ_0\n- **Update accumulators:**\n  - Compute eq(ξ_j, ρ_0) and multiply into `eq_r_acc`\n  - Compute next evaluation: `prev_s_eval = eq_r_acc × interpolate_quadratic(s'_vals, ρ_0)`\n- **Return:** r_0\n\n**Rounds 1..(round-1) (Subsequent Sumcheck Rounds):**\n\nTwo strategies, selected by `choose_round_strategy`:\n\n##### **Strategy 1: Fold-Eval (Default/Fallback)**\n\nFor each remaining variable in `xi_prev[1..]`:\n\nA. **Ping-pong scheduling** (lines 725-819):\n   - `BufferScheduler` decides: LayerToWork, WorkToLayer, InPlace_Layer, or InPlace_Work\n   - Goal: preserve `layer` for next GKR round's reverts (non-last rounds)\n\nB. **Fused Kernel Launch:**\n\n   **Out-of-place variant** (`do_fused_sumcheck_round`):\n   ```\n   Kernel: compute_round_and_fold_kernel\n   ```\n   - Reads: `src_pq_buffer` (size 2×pq_size), folding info from `r_prev`\n   - Writes: `dst_pq_buffer` (size pq_size), `block_sums`\n   - Fuses two operations:\n     1. **Fold** by r_prev: each pair → `even + r_prev × (odd - even)`, halving buffer\n     2. **Compute** s'_t(1) and s'_t(2) from freshly folded values\n   - No __restrict__ needed (distinct read/write buffers)\n\n   **In-place variant** (`do_fused_sumcheck_round_inplace`):\n   ```\n   Kernel: compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel (8.8ms, 1370 instances)\n   ```\n   - Reads and writes same buffer (first half written from both halves read)\n   - Same logic as out-of-place\n\nC. **CPU Work (inside `observe_and_update`):**\n   - D2H copy of `d_sum_evals` (2 EF values)\n   - Reconstruct s(1), s(2), s(3) as in Round 0\n   - Observe in transcript, sample ρ_t, update accumulators\n\nD. **Buffer progression:**\n   - `pq_size >>= 1` for next iteration\n   - Data location tracked by `scheduler.data_in_work_buffer`\n\n**Final fold** (after last sumcheck round, lines 795-819):\n   ```\n   Kernel: fold_ef_columns_kernel (if needed)\n   ```\n   - Standalone fold on final pq_size (no fused compute)\n   - No block reduction needed\n\n---\n\n##### **Strategy 2: Precompute-M (Lines 821-1054)**\n\nFor windows of w=3 sumcheck rounds (if conditions met):\n\n**Step 1: Build M matrix** (lines 909-926):\n```\nKernel: precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<inline_fold={true,false}> (45ms, 14 instances)\n+ precompute_m_reduce_partials_kernel\n```\n- Option A (inline_fold=true, first window): Folds by r_prev while building\n- Option B (inline_fold=false, subsequent): Assumes already folded\n- Computes M_{α,β}[u,v] for α,β ∈ {0,1} and u,v ∈ {0,1}^w\n- Parallelized by tail dimension (rem_n - w variables)\n- Each block produces partial M block, then reduce to final M matrix\n\n**Step 2: Eval w rounds** (lines 928-966):\n```\nKernel: precompute_m_eval_round_kernel\n```\n- Small kernel operating on 4^w = 64 element M matrix\n- For each round t ∈ [0, w):\n  - Compute eq_r_prefix from window challenges sampled so far\n  - Compute eq_suffix from remaining xi values\n  - Extract s'_t(1) and s'_t(2) from M using Lagrange/multilinearity\n  \n**CPU Work (inside loop):**\n   - **D2H Copy #4+**: Transfer `d_sum_evals` (2 EF)\n   - Reconstruct s(1), s(2), s(3)\n   - Observe in transcript, sample r_t\n   - Update `eq_r_acc` and `prev_s_eval`\n   - Accumulate challenges: `window_rs.push(r)`\n\n**Step 3: Multifold** (lines 968-1000):\n```\nKernel: multifold_kernel<4> (47ms, 14 instances)\n```\n- Folds w (or w+1 with pending) coordinates at once\n- Computes eq_r_window = MLE table of deferred challenges\n- Reads from layer_read_ptr (or active_pq if not pending)\n- Writes to active_pq\n- Reduces pq_size by 2^w (or 2^(w+1) if pending)\n\n**Fallback to fold-eval** (lines 1002-1046):\n- Any remaining rounds (rem_n < threshold) use standard fold-eval\n\n---\n\n**End of GKR Round:**\n\nAfter all sumcheck rounds:\n- **D2H Copy #5+**: Copy final two values from active buffer (lines 1057-1060)\n  ```rust\n  pq_host[0] = copy_from_device(active, 0, ...)?;\n  pq_host[1] = copy_from_device(active, pq_size/2, ...)?;\n  ```\n- Store claims for next GKR round\n- Observe claims in transcript\n- Sample μ_j (next round's reduction factor)\n- Update `xi_prev = [μ_j, ...r_vec]`\n- Accumulate `sumcheck_polys`\n\n---\n\n### 3. **CPU Work Between Kernel Launches**\n\n**Per Kernel Launch (3 main tasks):**\n\n1. **Polynomial Reconstruction** (~1-10 µs per round)\n   - D2H copy of 2 EF values from GPU (16 bytes)\n   - Field arithmetic:\n     - Inversion: `(1 - xi_j)^{-1}`\n     - Lagrange interpolation on 3 points\n     - 2 multiplications + 1 addition for eq evaluation\n     - Quadratic interpolation for next `prev_s_eval`\n\n2. **Fiat-Shamir Transcript Operations** (~1-5 µs per round)\n   - `transcript.observe_ext(3 values)` — hash update\n   - `transcript.sample_ext()` — challenge derivation from hash state\n\n3. **Buffer Management** (~negligible)\n   - Update pointers, sizes, accumulators\n   - Scheduler decisions for ping-pong\n\n**Key Insight:** CPU work is dominated by Fiat-Shamir hashing and D2H copies. Field arithmetic on CPU is minimal.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Data Dependencies Between Rounds/Levels**\n\n**Within a GKR Round (Sumcheck):**\n- Round t depends on: challenge ρ_{t-1} sampled in round t-1\n- pq_buffer must be folded by ρ_{t-1} before computing round t\n- No backward dependencies (except revert, which happens at start)\n\n**Between GKR Rounds:**\n- Round j depends on: μ_j and ρ_vec from round j-1\n- Layer buffer must be reverted at start of round j to expose layer j's data\n- eq_buffer must be rebuilt from xi_prev[1..] (CPU side, no GPU dependency)\n\n**Kernel → Kernel Serialization:**\n- Each `observe_and_update` requires D2H copy before next kernel\n- Creates hard synchronization point (CPU stall waiting for GPU result)\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Where `compute_round_and_revert` vs `compute_round_and_fold_inplace` are Used**\n\n| Kernel | Usage | Rounds Affected |\n|--------|-------|-----------------|\n| `compute_round_and_revert` | First inner round (t=0) of GKR round, after tree revert | Every GKR round |\n| `compute_round_and_fold_inplace` | Subsequent inner rounds (t=1...) using in-place strategy | Fold-eval strategy, non-last rounds |\n| `compute_round_and_fold` (out-of-place) | Subsequent inner rounds using ping-pong strategy | Fold-eval strategy, last round |\n| `precompute_m_eval_round` | Each window eval round (t=0..w-1) | Precompute-M strategy |\n\n---\n\n### 6. **How `bit_rev_frac_build_k2` Relates to Tree Structure**\n\n- **What it does:** Fused bit-reversal + tree layers 0 & 1\n- **Input:** Leaves in natural order with alpha not yet applied\n- **Output:** First 2 tree layers (halving tree from N → N/2 → N/4 nodes) in bit-reversed layout\n- **Memory benefits:** Eliminates 1.5N reads (normal: 1 read per bitrev + 1 per layer; fused: 1 read for both)\n- **Relation to tree:** After this kernel, `layer` buffer holds:\n  - `[0..N/4)`: layer 2 nodes (parents of layer 1)\n  - `[N/4..N/2)`: layer 1 nodes (preserved for revert of layer 2)\n  - `[N/2..N)`: layer 0 (leaves with alpha, preserved for revert of layer 1)\n- **Next kernels:** Continue building from layer 2 upward with `frac_build_tree_two_layers`\n\n---\n\n### 7. **D2H Copy Locations**\n\n| Line | Location | Data | Size | Frequency |\n|------|----------|------|------|-----------|\n| 579 | Tree build | Root fraction (p, q) | 32 B | Once |\n| 599-600 | GKR round 1 init | Layer 1 children (4 EF) | 64 B | Once |\n| 1057-1060 | GKR round end | Final claims (4 EF) | 64 B | Per GKR round |\n| Inside `reconstruct_s_evals` | Per sumcheck round | s'(1), s'(2) | 32 B | **Per sumcheck round** |\n\n**Total D2H per GKR round j:** 32B (final claims) + 32B × (j-1) (sumcheck rounds) = O(j × 32B)\n\nFor a large j, this is ~512 bytes per sumcheck round, which is minimal bandwidth but creates synchronization serialization.\n\n---\n\n### Files Summary\n\n**Rust Orchestration:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs` (1185 lines)\n- `fractional_sumcheck_gpu`: Main entry point\n- Helper functions: `observe_and_update`, `reconstruct_s_evals`, `copy_from_device`, `reduce_to_single_evaluation`\n- Buffer scheduling: `BufferScheduler`, strategy selection\n\n**CUDA Kernels:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr.cu` (1452 lines)\n- `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel`: Bitrev + tree layers 0-1\n- `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel`: Fused pair of tree layers\n- `compute_round_and_revert_kernel`: Revert + first sumcheck round\n- `compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel`: Fused fold + compute (in-place)\n- `compute_round_and_fold_kernel`: Fused fold + compute (out-of-place)\n- `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel<bool>`: Build M matrix\n- `precompute_m_eval_round_kernel`: Evaluate M matrix for one round\n- `multifold_kernel<W>`: Multi-coordinate fold\n\n**Wrapper Layer:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` (lines 101-850)\n- C++ extern declarations and safe Rust wrappers\n\n**Documentation:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/docs/cuda-backend/gkr-prover.md` (347 lines)"}],"totalDurationMs":112290,"totalTokens":88705,"totalToolUseCount":36,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":916,"cache_read_input_tokens":83046,"output_tokens":4741,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":916},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4741,"cache_read_input_tokens":83046,"cache_creation_input_tokens":916,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":916,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":22,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":14,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
