{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PWXYBTwXqDd7WrffLV4tFj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Batch Stacking Scatter Kernel\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReplace the per-column loop in `stack_traces_into_expanded()` (`crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs:145-179`) with a single batched scatter kernel. The current loop issues ~106K individual CUDA API calls at APC 300 (one `cudaMemcpyAsync` or one `batch_expand_pad_wide` kernel launch per trace column). Each API call has ~1us CPU overhead, creating ~100ms of CPU-side stalling that doesn't exist at APC 0 (~4K columns). A single kernel launch that processes all column copies in parallel eliminates this overhead.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Entry point\n10\t\n11\t`stacked_commit()` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs:48` calls either:\n12\t- Path A (`cache_stacked_matrix=true`): `stack_traces()` → `stack_traces_into_expanded(layout, traces, &mut q_evals, height)` (line 126)\n13\t- Path B (`cache_stacked_matrix=false`): `rs_code_matrix()` → `stack_traces_into_expanded(layout, traces, &mut codewords, codeword_height)` (line 219)\n14\t\n15\tBoth paths call the same function with different `padded_height`.\n16\t\n17\t### The loop (`stack_traces_into_expanded`, lines 134-181)\n18\t\n19\t```rust\n20\tbuffer.fill_zero()?;                              // single kernel: zeros entire stacked buffer\n21\tfor (mat_idx, j, s) in &layout.sorted_cols {       // iterates over ALL columns from ALL AIRs\n22\t    let start = s.col_idx * padded_height + s.row_idx;\n23\t    let trace = traces[*mat_idx];\n24\t    let s_len = s.len(l_skip);\n25\t    if s.log_height() >= l_skip {\n26\t        // D2D memcpy: copy s_len elements from trace column j to stacked position\n27\t        cuda_memcpy::<true, true>(dst, src, s_len * size_of::<F>())?;\n28\t    } else {\n29\t        // Strided expansion: write trace.height() elements with stride into stacked position\n30\t        batch_expand_pad_wide(dst, src, trace.height() as u32, stride as u32, 1)?;\n31\t    }\n32\t}\n33\t```\n34\t\n35\tAt APC 300: `layout.sorted_cols` has ~106K entries (106,842 total columns across 623 AIR instances, 2 segments). Each iteration issues one CUDA API call:\n36\t- ~95K `cudaMemcpyAsync` calls (D2D, ~1us CPU overhead each)\n37\t- ~11K `batch_expand_pad_wide` kernel launches (~5us CPU overhead each)\n38\t- Total CPU overhead: ~95K × 1us + 11K × 5us ≈ ~150ms\n39\t\n40\tAt APC 0: ~4K entries → ~4ms overhead (negligible).\n41\t\n42\t### Why this is slow\n43\t\n44\tThe GPU work per column is tiny (copying 8-256 elements = 32-1024 bytes). The actual GPU compute time for all copies combined is ~3ms. The bottleneck is purely CPU-side: iterating over 106K entries and making 106K CUDA runtime API calls.\n45\t\n46\t## Changes\n47\t\n48\t### Change 1: New CUDA kernel — `stack_columns_kernel`\n49\t\n50\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/matrix.cu`\n51\t\n52\tAdd a new kernel and its extern \"C\" wrapper:\n53\t\n54\t```cuda\n55\tstruct StackColDesc {\n56\t    const Fp* src;       // source pointer (device memory)\n57\t    Fp* dst;             // destination pointer (device memory)\n58\t    uint32_t height;     // number of source elements to copy\n59\t    uint32_t stride;     // 1 for plain copy, >1 for strided expansion\n60\t};\n61\t\n62\t__global__ void stack_columns_kernel(\n63\t    const StackColDesc* descs,\n64\t    uint32_t num_descs\n65\t) {\n66\t    uint32_t desc_idx = blockIdx.x;\n67\t    if (desc_idx >= num_descs) return;\n68\t\n69\t    const StackColDesc& d = descs[desc_idx];\n70\t    for (uint32_t i = threadIdx.x; i < d.height; i += blockDim.x) {\n71\t        d.dst[i * d.stride] = d.src[i];\n72\t    }\n73\t}\n74\t\n75\textern \"C\" int _stack_columns(\n76\t    const StackColDesc* descs,\n77\t    uint32_t num_descs\n78\t) {\n79\t    if (num_descs == 0) return 0;\n80\t    dim3 grid(num_descs);\n81\t    dim3 block(256);\n82\t    stack_columns_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(descs, num_descs);\n83\t    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n84\t}\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\t**Why**: Each CUDA block handles one column descriptor. Thread 0..255 within the block cooperatively copy elements from `src` to `dst` with the given stride. For a column with `height=64` and `stride=1`, 64 of the 256 threads do work. For `height=8` and `stride=4`, 8 threads do work. The kernel is launch-overhead-dominated (not compute-limited), so 256 threads per block is sufficient.\n88\t\n89\t**Grid dimension limit**: `num_descs` can be up to ~53K per segment. CUDA grid.x supports up to 2^31-1, so this is fine.\n90\t\n91\t### Change 2: Rust FFI wrapper\n92\t\n93\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/matrix.rs`\n94\t\n95\tAdd FFI binding and safe wrapper:\n96\t\n97\t```rust\n98\t#[repr(C)]\n99\tpub struct StackColDesc {\n100\t    pub src: *const F,\n101\t    pub dst: *mut F,\n102\t    pub height: u32,\n103\t    pub stride: u32,\n104\t}\n105\t\n106\t// SAFETY: StackColDesc contains device pointers that are only dereferenced on GPU\n107\tunsafe impl Send for StackColDesc {}\n108\tunsafe impl Sync for StackColDesc {}\n109\t\n110\textern \"C\" {\n111\t    fn _stack_columns(descs: *const StackColDesc, num_descs: u32) -> i32;\n112\t}\n113\t\n114\tpub unsafe fn stack_columns(descs: &DeviceBuffer<StackColDesc>) -> Result<(), CudaError> {\n115\t    CudaError::from_result(_stack_columns(descs.as_ptr(), descs.len() as u32))\n116\t}\n117\t```\n118\t\n119\t**Why**: The `#[repr(C)]` struct matches the CUDA struct layout. The DeviceBuffer ensures descriptors are uploaded to GPU memory before the kernel reads them. Send+Sync are needed because the struct holds raw pointers that are only used on the GPU side.\n120\t\n121\t### Change 3: Restructure `stack_traces_into_expanded()`\n122\t\n123\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs`\n124\t\n125\tReplace the per-column loop (lines 145-179) with a two-phase approach:\n126\t\n127\t```rust\n128\tpub(crate) fn stack_traces_into_expanded(\n129\t    layout: &StackedLayout,\n130\t    traces: &[&DeviceMatrix<F>],\n131\t    buffer: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,\n132\t    padded_height: usize,\n133\t) -> Result<(), StackTracesError> {\n134\t    let l_skip = layout.l_skip();\n135\t    buffer.fill_zero().map_err(StackTracesError::FillZero)?;\n136\t\n137\t    // Phase 1: Build descriptor array on host\n138\t    let mut descs: Vec<StackColDesc> = Vec::with_capacity(layout.sorted_cols.len());\n139\t    for (mat_idx, j, s) in &layout.sorted_cols {\n140\t        let start = s.col_idx * padded_height + s.row_idx;\n141\t        let trace = traces[*mat_idx];\n142\t        let (src, height, stride) = if s.log_height() >= l_skip {\n143\t            let s_len = s.len(l_skip);\n144\t            let src = unsafe { trace.buffer().as_ptr().add(*j * s_len) };\n145\t            (src, s_len as u32, 1u32)\n146\t        } else {\n147\t            let stride = s.stride(l_skip);\n148\t            let src = unsafe { trace.buffer().as_ptr().add(*j * trace.height()) };\n149\t            (src, trace.height() as u32, stride as u32)\n150\t        };\n151\t        let dst = unsafe { buffer.as_mut_ptr().add(start) };\n152\t        descs.push(StackColDesc { src, dst, height, stride });\n153\t    }\n154\t\n155\t    // Phase 2: Upload descriptors and launch single kernel\n156\t    if !descs.is_empty() {\n157\t        let d_descs = descs.to_device().map_err(StackTracesError::DescriptorUpload)?;\n158\t        unsafe { stack_columns(&d_descs).map_err(StackTracesError::StackColumns)? };\n159\t    }\n160\t    Ok(())\n161\t}\n162\t```\n163\t\n164\t**Why**: Phase 1 iterates on the CPU to build the descriptor Vec — this has ~0 CUDA overhead per iteration (just pointer arithmetic and Vec::push). Phase 2 uploads the descriptor array to GPU with a single H2D copy (~2.5MB for 106K descriptors) and launches one kernel.\n165\t\n166\t### Change 4: Add error variant\n167\t\n168\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs` (or wherever `StackTracesError` is defined)\n169\t\n170\tAdd two new variants to `StackTracesError`:\n171\t- `DescriptorUpload(MemCopyError)` — for the H2D upload of the descriptor array\n172\t- `StackColumns(CudaError)` — for the scatter kernel launch\n173\t\n174\t### Change 5: Add import for `to_device` trait\n175\t\n176\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs`\n177\t\n178\tAdd `use openvm_cuda_common::copy::MemCopyH2D;` to import the `to_device()` method on `[StackColDesc]`.\n179\t\n180\tThe `MemCopyH2D` impl for `[T]` requires `T: Copy`. `StackColDesc` with `#[repr(C)]` and all `Copy` fields is `Copy`, so this works. If `MemCopyH2D` requires `T: Pod` or similar, implement the necessary trait for `StackColDesc`.\n181\t\n182\t## Invariants\n183\t\n184\t1. **Correctness**: The stacked buffer must contain identical data after the optimization. Each column's source data must be written to the same `(col_idx * padded_height + row_idx)` offset with the same stride.\n185\t2. **Zero padding**: The `fill_zero()` call must remain. The scatter kernel only writes non-zero elements; zeros between stacked columns are provided by fill_zero.\n186\t3. **Memory safety**: All source pointers (`src`) come from `traces[mat_idx].buffer()` which are valid GPU allocations. All destination pointers (`dst`) point into `buffer` which is a single contiguous GPU allocation. No overlapping writes (the stacking layout guarantees non-overlapping column positions).\n187\t4. **Ordering**: The kernel does not require any ordering between descriptors. All writes are to non-overlapping regions, so concurrent execution is safe.\n188\t5. **Existing callers**: Both Path A (`stack_traces()`, line 126) and Path B (`rs_code_matrix()`, line 219) call `stack_traces_into_expanded()` and will automatically benefit.\n189\t\n190\t## Measurement Plan\n191\t\n192\t### Before/After comparison\n193\t\n194\tRun `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300} before and after the change.\n195\t\n196\t### Primary metrics (from spec.py)\n197\t\n198\t| Metric | Target |\n199\t|--------|--------|\n200\t| Trace Commit (APC 300) | < 340ms (16%+ improvement from 404ms) |\n201\t| STARK excl trace (APC 300) | < 1670ms (3.5%+ improvement from 1731ms) |\n202\t| STARK excl trace (APC 0) | No regression (< 2200ms, within noise of 2148ms) |\n203\t| All other sub-metrics (APC 300) | No regression (within ±5% of current) |\n204\t\n205\t### Validation\n206\t\n207\tRun `run_pairing.sh` for all three APC configs. All must complete prove+verify successfully.\n208\t\n209\t### Profiling\n210\t\n211\tRun with nsight to verify:\n212\t- `batch_expand_pad_wide_kernel` instances drop from ~12K to 0\n213\t- New `stack_columns_kernel` appears with ~1-2 instances per segment\n214\t- Total kernel launch count within Trace Commit is significantly reduced\n215\t\n216\t## Rollback Criteria\n217\t\n218\tRevert if ANY of the following:\n219\t- Trace Commit at APC 300 improves by less than 10% (< 40ms savings)\n220\t- STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by more than 3% (> 64ms increase)\n221\t- Any APC config fails prove+verify\n222\t- Memory usage increases by more than 10MB (descriptor array overhead)\n223\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011cVGJYxsFhcbEPdAFJ8jJc","session_id":"7b26f810-a2da-49ad-94ae-b745d8ecf3f5","uuid":"78e14ea9-518b-40d3-843c-a7e025947ab6","timestamp":"2026-04-12T23:57:09.208Z"}
