{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GdV5yGUbdohoefMRrGxq5Q","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Batch Stacking Scatter Kernel\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReplace the per-column loop in `stack_traces_into_expanded()` (`crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs:145-179`) with a single batched scatter kernel. The current loop issues ~106K individual CUDA API calls at APC 300 (one `cudaMemcpyAsync` or one `batch_expand_pad_wide` kernel launch per trace column). Each API call has ~1-5us CPU overhead, creating an estimated ~100-150ms of CPU-side stalling across 2 segments that doesn't exist at APC 0 (~4K columns). A single kernel launch per invocation eliminates this overhead.\n6\t\n7\tThe 106K columns are split across 2 segments (~53K per segment). The function is called once per segment, so the savings are approximately ~50-75ms per segment for a total of ~100-150ms.\n8\t\n9\t## Current Code Path\n10\t\n11\t### Entry point\n12\t\n13\t`stacked_commit()` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs:48` calls either:\n14\t- Path A (`cache_stacked_matrix=true`): `stack_traces()` → `stack_traces_into_expanded(layout, traces, &mut q_evals, height)` (line 126)\n15\t- Path B (`cache_stacked_matrix=false`): `rs_code_matrix()` → `stack_traces_into_expanded(layout, traces, &mut codewords, codeword_height)` (line 219)\n16\t\n17\tBoth paths call the same function with different `padded_height`.\n18\t\n19\t**Benchmark configuration**: The default `GpuProverConfig` has `cache_stacked_matrix: false`, so the benchmark uses Path B. With Path B, `stack_traces_into_expanded` is called inside `rs_code_matrix` with `padded_height = codeword_height` (typically `height * 2^log_blowup`). Path A's `stack_traces()` is not called. The optimization benefits Path B directly.\n20\t\n21\t### The loop (`stack_traces_into_expanded`, lines 134-181)\n22\t\n23\t```rust\n24\tbuffer.fill_zero()?;                              // single kernel: zeros entire stacked buffer\n25\tfor (mat_idx, j, s) in &layout.sorted_cols {       // iterates over ALL columns from ALL AIRs\n26\t    let start = s.col_idx * padded_height + s.row_idx;\n27\t    let trace = traces[*mat_idx];\n28\t    let s_len = s.len(l_skip);\n29\t    if s.log_height() >= l_skip {\n30\t        // D2D memcpy: copy s_len elements from trace column j to stacked position\n31\t        cuda_memcpy::<true, true>(dst, src, s_len * size_of::<F>())?;\n32\t    } else {\n33\t        // Strided expansion: write trace.height() elements with stride into stacked position\n34\t        batch_expand_pad_wide(dst, src, trace.height() as u32, stride as u32, 1)?;\n35\t    }\n36\t}\n37\t```\n38\t\n39\tAt APC 300: `layout.sorted_cols` has ~106K entries total (106,842 columns across 623 AIR instances, split across 2 segments — ~53K per segment). Each iteration issues one CUDA API call:\n40\t- ~95K `cudaMemcpyAsync` calls total (D2D, ~1us CPU overhead each)\n41\t- ~11K `batch_expand_pad_wide` kernel launches total (~5us CPU overhead each)\n42\t- Total CPU overhead across both segments: ~95K × 1us + 11K × 5us ≈ ~150ms\n43\t- Per segment: ~75ms\n44\t\n45\tNote on `batch_expand_pad_wide` parameter usage: the stacking code \"abuses\" this function with `width=trace.height()`, `padded_height=stride`, `height=1` — treating each source element as a separate \"column\" to create a strided output pattern. The scatter kernel replaces this with a simple `dst[i * stride] = src[i]` loop.\n46\t\n47\tAt APC 0: ~4K entries total → ~4ms overhead (negligible).\n48\t\n49\t### Why this is slow\n50\t\n51\tThe GPU work per column is tiny (copying 8-256 elements = 32-1024 bytes). The actual GPU compute time for all copies combined is ~3ms. The bottleneck is purely CPU-side: iterating over 106K entries and making 106K CUDA runtime API calls.\n52\t\n53\t## Changes\n54\t\n55\t### Change 1: New CUDA kernel — `stack_columns_kernel`\n56\t\n57\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/matrix.cu`\n58\t\n59\tAdd a new kernel and its extern \"C\" wrapper:\n60\t\n61\t```cuda\n62\tstruct StackColDesc {\n63\t    const Fp* src;       // source pointer (device memory)\n64\t    Fp* dst;             // destination pointer (device memory)\n65\t    uint32_t height;     // number of source elements to copy\n66\t    uint32_t stride;     // 1 for plain copy, >1 for strided expansion\n67\t};\n68\t\n69\t__global__ void stack_columns_kernel(\n70\t    const StackColDesc* descs,\n71\t    uint32_t num_descs\n72\t) {\n73\t    uint32_t desc_idx = blockIdx.x;\n74\t    if (desc_idx >= num_descs) return;\n75\t\n76\t    const StackColDesc& d = descs[desc_idx];\n77\t    for (uint32_t i = threadIdx.x; i < d.height; i += blockDim.x) {\n78\t        d.dst[i * d.stride] = d.src[i];\n79\t    }\n80\t}\n81\t\n82\textern \"C\" int _stack_columns(\n83\t    const StackColDesc* descs,\n84\t    uint32_t num_descs\n85\t) {\n86\t    if (num_descs == 0) return 0;\n87\t    dim3 grid(num_descs);\n88\t    dim3 block(256);\n89\t    stack_columns_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(descs, num_descs);\n90\t    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n91\t}\n92\t```\n93\t\n94\t**Why**: Each CUDA block handles one column descriptor. Thread 0..255 within the block cooperatively copy elements from `src` to `dst` with the given stride. For a column with `height=64` and `stride=1`, 64 of the 256 threads do work. For `height=8` and `stride=4`, 8 threads do work. The kernel is launch-overhead-dominated (not compute-limited), so 256 threads per block is sufficient.\n95\t\n96\t**Grid dimension limit**: `num_descs` can be up to ~53K per segment. CUDA grid.x supports up to 2^31-1, so this is fine.\n97\t\n98\t### Change 2: Rust FFI wrapper\n99\t\n100\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/matrix.rs`\n101\t\n102\tAdd FFI binding and safe wrapper:\n103\t\n104\t```rust\n105\t#[repr(C)]\n106\tpub struct StackColDesc {\n107\t    pub src: *const F,\n108\t    pub dst: *mut F,\n109\t    pub height: u32,\n110\t    pub stride: u32,\n111\t}\n112\t\n113\t// SAFETY: StackColDesc contains device pointers that are only dereferenced on GPU\n114\tunsafe impl Send for StackColDesc {}\n115\tunsafe impl Sync for StackColDesc {}\n116\t\n117\textern \"C\" {\n118\t    fn _stack_columns(descs: *const StackColDesc, num_descs: u32) -> i32;\n119\t}\n120\t\n121\tpub unsafe fn stack_columns(descs: &DeviceBuffer<StackColDesc>) -> Result<(), CudaError> {\n122\t    CudaError::from_result(_stack_columns(descs.as_ptr(), descs.len() as u32))\n123\t}\n124\t```\n125\t\n126\t**Why**: The `#[repr(C)]` struct matches the CUDA struct layout. The DeviceBuffer ensures descriptors are uploaded to GPU memory before the kernel reads them. Send+Sync are needed because the struct holds raw pointers that are only used on the GPU side.\n127\t\n128\t### Change 3: Restructure `stack_traces_into_expanded()`\n129\t\n130\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs`\n131\t\n132\tReplace the per-column loop (lines 145-179) with a two-phase approach:\n133\t\n134\t```rust\n135\tpub(crate) fn stack_traces_into_expanded(\n136\t    layout: &StackedLayout,\n137\t    traces: &[&DeviceMatrix<F>],\n138\t    buffer: &mut DeviceBuffer<F>,\n139\t    padded_height: usize,\n140\t) -> Result<(), StackTracesError> {\n141\t    let l_skip = layout.l_skip();\n142\t    debug_assert_eq!(padded_height % layout.height(), 0);\n143\t    debug_assert_eq!(buffer.len() % padded_height, 0);\n144\t    debug_assert_eq!(buffer.len() / padded_height, layout.width());\n145\t    buffer.fill_zero().map_err(StackTracesError::FillZero)?;\n146\t\n147\t    // Phase 1: Build descriptor array on host (CPU-only, no CUDA API calls)\n148\t    let mut descs: Vec<StackColDesc> = Vec::with_capacity(layout.sorted_cols.len());\n149\t    for (mat_idx, j, s) in &layout.sorted_cols {\n150\t        let start = s.col_idx * padded_height + s.row_idx;\n151\t        let trace = traces[*mat_idx];\n152\t        let s_len = s.len(l_skip);\n153\t        debug_assert_eq!(trace.height(), 1 << s.log_height());\n154\t        let (src, height, stride) = if s.log_height() >= l_skip {\n155\t            debug_assert_eq!(trace.height(), s_len);\n156\t            let src = unsafe { trace.buffer().as_ptr().add(*j * s_len) };\n157\t            (src, s_len as u32, 1u32)\n158\t        } else {\n159\t            let stride = s.stride(l_skip);\n160\t            debug_assert_eq!(stride * trace.height(), s_len);\n161\t            let src = unsafe { trace.buffer().as_ptr().add(*j * trace.height()) };\n162\t            (src, trace.height() as u32, stride as u32)\n163\t        };\n164\t        let dst = unsafe { buffer.as_mut_ptr().add(start) };\n165\t        descs.push(StackColDesc { src, dst, height, stride });\n166\t    }\n167\t\n168\t    // Phase 2: Upload descriptors to GPU and launch single scatter kernel\n169\t    if !descs.is_empty() {\n170\t        let d_descs = descs.to_device().map_err(StackTracesError::DescriptorUpload)?;\n171\t        unsafe { stack_columns(&d_descs).map_err(StackTracesError::StackColumns)? };\n172\t    }\n173\t    Ok(())\n174\t}\n175\t```\n176\t\n177\t**Why**: Phase 1 iterates on the CPU to build the descriptor Vec — this has ~0 CUDA overhead per iteration (just pointer arithmetic and Vec::push). Phase 2 uploads the descriptor array to GPU with a single H2D copy (~2.5MB for ~53K descriptors per segment) and launches one kernel.\n178\t\n179\t**Memory safety for H2D copy**: `to_device()` uses `cudaMemcpyAsync` on pageable (non-pinned) host memory. For pageable memory, the CUDA runtime internally stages the data before returning, so the host Vec can safely go out of scope after the call. The `fill_zero`, H2D copy, and scatter kernel all run on `cudaStreamPerThread`, so they execute in order on the GPU.\n180\t\n181\t### Change 4: Add error variants\n182\t\n183\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/error.rs` (where `StackTracesError` is defined, lines 130-137)\n184\t\n185\tAdd two new variants to `StackTracesError`:\n186\t- `DescriptorUpload(MemCopyError)` — for the H2D upload of the descriptor array (uses explicit `.map_err()`, not `#[from]`)\n187\t- `StackColumns(CudaError)` — for the scatter kernel launch\n188\t\n189\tThe existing `MemCopy(#[from] MemCopyError)` and `BatchExpandPadWide(CudaError)` variants become dead code after this change. Remove them to avoid confusion.\n190\t\n191\t### Change 5: Update imports\n192\t\n193\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs`\n194\t\n195\t- Add `use openvm_cuda_common::copy::MemCopyH2D;` to import the `to_device()` method on `[StackColDesc]`.\n196\t- Add `use crate::cuda::matrix::{StackColDesc, stack_columns};` to import the new FFI types.\n197\t- Remove unused imports: `cuda_memcpy` from line 5, `batch_expand_pad_wide` from line 16 (both are no longer called).\n198\t\n199\tThe `MemCopyH2D` impl for `[T]` requires `T: Copy`. `StackColDesc` with `#[repr(C)]` and all `Copy` fields derives `Copy` automatically. If `MemCopyH2D` requires `T: Pod` or similar, implement the necessary trait for `StackColDesc`.\n200\t\n201\t## Invariants\n202\t\n203\t1. **Correctness**: The stacked buffer must contain identical data after the optimization. Each column's source data must be written to the same `(col_idx * padded_height + row_idx)` offset with the same stride.\n204\t2. **Zero padding**: The `fill_zero()` call must remain. The scatter kernel only writes non-zero elements; zeros between stacked columns are provided by fill_zero.\n205\t3. **Memory safety**: All source pointers (`src`) come from `traces[mat_idx].buffer()` which are valid GPU allocations. All destination pointers (`dst`) point into `buffer` which is a single contiguous GPU allocation. No overlapping writes (the stacking layout guarantees non-overlapping column positions).\n206\t4. **Ordering**: The kernel does not require any ordering between descriptors. All writes are to non-overlapping regions, so concurrent execution is safe.\n207\t5. **Existing callers**: Both Path A (`stack_traces()`, line 126) and Path B (`rs_code_matrix()`, line 219) call `stack_traces_into_expanded()` and will automatically benefit.\n208\t\n209\t## Measurement Plan\n210\t\n211\t### Before/After comparison\n212\t\n213\tRun `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300} before and after the change.\n214\t\n215\t### Primary metrics (from spec.py)\n216\t\n217\tThe 404ms \"Trace Commit\" at APC 300 is the sum across 2 segments (233ms + 166ms from `stacked_commit_time_ms`). Each segment calls `stack_traces_into_expanded()` once with ~53K columns. The savings are per-segment: ~50-75ms per segment, ~100-150ms total.\n218\t\n219\t| Metric | Target |\n220\t|--------|--------|\n221\t| Trace Commit (APC 300) | < 330ms (18%+ improvement from 404ms) |\n222\t| STARK excl trace (APC 300) | < 1660ms (4%+ improvement from 1731ms) |\n223\t| STARK excl trace (APC 0) | No regression (< 2200ms, within noise of 2148ms) |\n224\t| All other sub-metrics (APC 300) | No regression (within ±5% of current) |\n225\t\n226\t### Validation\n227\t\n228\tRun `run_pairing.sh` for all three APC configs. All must complete prove+verify successfully.\n229\t\n230\t### Profiling\n231\t\n232\tRun with nsight to verify:\n233\t- `batch_expand_pad_wide_kernel` instances drop from ~12K to 0\n234\t- New `stack_columns_kernel` appears with ~1-2 instances per segment\n235\t- Total kernel launch count within Trace Commit is significantly reduced\n236\t\n237\t## Rollback Criteria\n238\t\n239\tRevert if ANY of the following:\n240\t- Trace Commit at APC 300 improves by less than 10% (< 40ms savings)\n241\t- STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by more than 3% (> 64ms increase)\n242\t- Any APC config fails prove+verify\n243\t- Memory usage increases by more than 10MB (descriptor array overhead)\n244\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XVrvupcMHZGasj5E377JPx","session_id":"7b26f810-a2da-49ad-94ae-b745d8ecf3f5","uuid":"4272570f-65a0-4a90-8fb8-ebffa7dddcb0","timestamp":"2026-04-13T00:02:29.638Z"}
